    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5c368899-d410-5f0d-b7ce-ddc6785e892a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=-x+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$\n\n- **II.** $x\\in (0.5,\\infty)$\n\n- **III.** $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $-1$ and $y$-intercept $1,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124474/plots/2c8d228588192f516fdebd138180010cf435049b3fb17c9c7f245910d2fcb452-0.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (0.5,\\infty)$ and $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "b8cf5e98-09e9-5b1c-9eb2-668937802d29",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
